{
  "teamId": 143,
  "year": 2019,
  "narrative": "The 2019 Philadelphia Phillies arrived with sky-high expectations. Bryce Harper had just signed the richest contract in baseball history, joining a roster that also added J.T. Realmuto, Andrew McCutchen, and Jean Segura in a bold offseason spending spree. Citizens Bank Park buzzed with genuine playoff optimism for the first time in years. The season began with a modest promise — a perfect March, then a .500 April that felt like a foundation being laid rather than a ceiling being approached.\n\nMay was the Phillies' best month, a 17-11 stretch powered by timely hitting from Maikel Franco, clutch home runs from Rhys Hoskins, and the new faces delivering on their billing. Realmuto and McCutchen chipped in key hits, and the lineup looked like what the front office had envisioned. But June proved to be the season's turning point in the worst way — an 11-16 collapse driven largely by a bullpen that couldn't hold leads. Héctor Neris became the story for all the wrong reasons, surrendering walk-off blows and late-inning homers that turned winnable games into losses. The closer's repeated implosions in June and into July poisoned what could have been a playoff push.\n\nJuly and August were a back-and-forth grind. Harper delivered a stunning grand slam in August and a go-ahead double against the Dodgers, and Corey Dickerson — a midseason addition — provided a spark, but the bullpen remained a revolving door of heartbreak. Neris gave up homers to Juan Soto, Matt Beaty, Josh Bell, and Todd Frazier in high-leverage moments that swung games decisively away from Philadelphia. The Phillies simply could not protect leads, and a run differential of minus-20 for the full season told the honest story: this was a team that played just well enough to tease its fans.\n\nSeptember sealed the Phillies' fate. A 12-16 final month, capped by Trea Turner's grand slam off Jared Hughes in late September, officially buried any lingering wild card hopes. The final record of 81-81 was almost poetically fitting — a team that was exactly as good as it was flawed, never able to separate itself from mediocrity. Harper was electric in flashes, the offense posted 215 home runs, but a 4.53 ERA and a bullpen that hemorrhaged big moments made .500 the ceiling rather than the floor. Philadelphia would have to wait at least another year.",
